# ATO lodgment rounding (general — any taxpayer)

## Official ATO rule (BAS + tax returns)

- Enter **whole dollar** amounts on BAS / activity statements and income tax returns.
- **Leave cents out** and **do not round up** to the next dollar (truncate / `Math.trunc`).
- Keep **ledger books in cents**; round only when copying into ATO labels.
- Company tax return: same — no cents on return labels.
- Individual myTax labels: same whole-dollar practice for amounts entered on the return.
- **Exception:** PAYG withholding *formula tables* (Schedule 1) use nearest dollar with 50c up — payroll calculators only.

Sources: ATO “BAS and GST tips” / “Complete your BAS”; company tax return instructions.

## Book vs bank (BAS refund class)

1. Ledger ÷11 (or accounts method) may show a GST credit of e.g. **$18.45**.
2. Lodged BAS label uses whole dollars → **$18**.
3. ATO refunds **$18** into the bank (`NON_TAXABLE_ATO_GST_REFUND`).
4. Balance Sheet: Cash = banked **$18**; clear cents gap (**$0.45**) from Total RE so Assets = L+E.
5. SELPIC Q3 $18 is **one example** of this class — never hard-code $18 as the only banked amount.

Helpers: `roundAtoWholeDollars`, `atoGstRefundRoundingGap(banked, ledgerEstimate)`.

## Income tax year-end

- CTR / myTax copy sheets must use `roundAtoWholeDollars` on label amounts.
- Ledger CTR profit for Balance Sheet equity may stay in cents; lodgment sheet is whole dollars.
- Do not “fix” a $0.45–$0.99 BS gap by changing GST Payable or cash Net — clear via ATO cents-left-out rounding when banked ≠ ledger estimate.

## Wrong vs right

| Topic | Wrong | Right |
|-------|-------|--------|
| BAS labels | `Math.round` to cents or nearest $ | Whole $ truncate |
| Banked refund | Put $18.45 in Cash | Banked whole $ from statement |
| Gap | Hard-code SELPIC $18 only | `estimate − banked` for any user |
| Payroll W tables | Truncate | Nearest $ (50c up) — separate |
